  "id": "8af51da4-39e1-52d7-8cd4-fea4da412be5",
  "slug": "manti-armenian-dumplings",
  "name": "Manti (Armenian Dumplings)",
  "recipe_type": "side",
  "brief_description": "Manti are classic Armenian dumplings made with a pasta-like dough and filled with savory ground beef. Any plant-based alternative works, too,",
  "cuisine": "Middle Eastern",
  "instructions": [
    {
      "position": 1,
      "text": "Make dough: Combine 3/4 of the flour, egg, olive oil, salt, and water in a medium bowl. Mix with a fork, just like when making a pasta dough, until ingredients just come together. Continue mixing everything by hand into a shaggy dough. Turn out mixture onto a floured surface and keep kneading until dough is smooth. Add more flour as needed. Cover and allow dough to rest on the counter for 30 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 2,
      "text": "Meanwhile, make filling: Combine ground beef, onion, parsley, smoked paprika, kosher salt, pepper, and cayenne in a bowl. Mix with a fork, then use your hands until mixture is well combined. Cover and chill in the refrigerator."
    },
    {
      "position": 3,
      "text": "Preheat the oven to 400 degrees C (200 degrees C). Grease a cast iron skillet with olive oil."
    },
    {
      "position": 4,
      "text": "Lightly flour a work surface and set the dough on top. Cut dough in half and roll one half out as thin as possible into a large rectangle. Cut dough first into strips, then into 20 small rectangles, about 1.5 x 1.75 inches each. Reserve dough scraps in case there is leftover filling to make extra manti. Repeat with second half."
    },
    {
      "position": 5,
      "text": "Use a teaspoon to portion the filling, about 1/2 to 1 teaspoon per manti, and set into the middle of each dough rectangle (you can also use your hands to portion out the filling as shown in the video)."
    },
    {
      "position": 6,
      "text": "Moisten the outer, shorter edges of each rectangle with a little water on your fingertips, pick up a rectangle, and trap the filling inside by pinching both ends of the dough together to create the classic \u201clittle man in the boat\u201d design. Press dumpling together so manti will sit up straight in the skillet, making sure the dough at the ends is as thin as it is around the filling."
    },
    {
      "position": 7,
      "text": "Set manti inside the prepared skillet with room between; dough will expand to about twice its size during cooking."
    },
    {
      "position": 8,
      "text": "Bake in the preheated oven on the center rack for 40 minutes."
    },
    {
      "position": 9,
      "text": "Meanwhile, make broth: Heat olive oil in a pot over medium-high heat and cook garlic until fragrant, 20 to 30 seconds. Add tomato sauce and stir quickly. Add chicken broth and season with salt, pepper, and cayenne."
    },
    {
      "position": 10,
      "text": "Bring tomato broth to a simmer and turn off the heat."
    },
    {
      "position": 11,
      "text": "Ladle tomato broth over the manti and return to the oven for 15 more minutes."
    }
  ],
  "servings": 6,
